WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:01.464
- Good morning, Believe Nation.

00:00:01.464 --> 00:00:02.789
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:02.789 --> 00:00:04.953
My one word is believe and I believe

00:00:04.953 --> 00:00:06.600
that entrepreneurs are going to

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.709
solve all of the world's major problems.

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:11.316
So, today we're going to learn how to not

00:00:11.316 --> 00:00:13.149
take no for an answer.

00:00:14.318 --> 00:00:16.847
Over to you, Ryan Seacrest.

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:18.371
(rooster crowing)

00:00:18.371 --> 00:00:20.954
(upbeat music)

00:00:27.913 --> 00:00:30.524
- What do you think the secret
to your success has been?

00:00:30.524 --> 00:00:32.433
- I think, for me,

00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:33.376
- Like if they were going to ask you,

00:00:33.376 --> 00:00:36.396
Ryan what can I do, what
would you tell them?

00:00:36.396 --> 00:00:39.698
- It is absolute focus and determination

00:00:39.698 --> 00:00:42.560
and a love for what you're pursuing.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.950
And I think they, as
I did, will be told no

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.616
a lot, and that no is something
you can't always listen to.

00:00:50.616 --> 00:00:54.414
Like, sometimes that no can become a yes.

00:00:54.414 --> 00:00:55.545
But, I think you've got to find out

00:00:55.545 --> 00:00:57.060
something you love and pursue it

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.089
with a lot of focus.

00:00:58.089 --> 00:00:59.530
- So, along your path to success you're

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:00.738
going to get a lot of no's.

00:01:00.738 --> 00:01:02.530
You get a lot of people saying no, no,

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.302
no, no, no, no, no.

00:01:04.302 --> 00:01:05.841
You can expect it.

00:01:05.841 --> 00:01:07.077
And, you know what?

00:01:07.077 --> 00:01:08.766
A lot of those no's are justified

00:01:08.766 --> 00:01:10.234
because when you are first starting

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:11.878
something, you're going to suck.

00:01:11.878 --> 00:01:14.527
Your quality, your
output is going to suck.

00:01:14.527 --> 00:01:15.570
It's not going to be very good because

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:16.505
you don't have the talent.

00:01:16.505 --> 00:01:17.338
You don't have the skills.

00:01:17.338 --> 00:01:18.758
You haven't put in the effort yet.

00:01:18.758 --> 00:01:20.646
And so, you can expect people to say no

00:01:20.646 --> 00:01:21.989
and it's not a malicious no, it's not,

00:01:21.989 --> 00:01:24.562
I want you to fail no.

00:01:24.562 --> 00:01:27.111
You just aren't providing
them with enough value yet.

00:01:27.111 --> 00:01:30.036
And, that's okay, but
if you have the passion

00:01:30.036 --> 00:01:31.781
for what you want to
do, this is a big dream,

00:01:31.781 --> 00:01:33.873
this is a big goal of yours, then you

00:01:33.873 --> 00:01:35.530
have to push through.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.624
There's this great clip
of Sylvester Stallone

00:01:38.624 --> 00:01:41.016
where he says I love getting no's,

00:01:41.016 --> 00:01:42.896
because a no just means a yes.

00:01:42.896 --> 00:01:44.264
He sees it as a challenge.

00:01:44.264 --> 00:01:46.128
The fact that he got a no is a challenge

00:01:46.128 --> 00:01:47.677
for him to want to go and push harder

00:01:47.677 --> 00:01:50.942
and go faster and do more and prove,

00:01:50.942 --> 00:01:52.577
prove to, prove to them but prove

00:01:52.577 --> 00:01:55.516
to himself that he's good enough.

00:01:55.516 --> 00:01:56.763
That he can do it.

00:01:56.763 --> 00:01:58.418
And so, I think it's really important

00:01:58.418 --> 00:02:01.933
that you use negativity, you use no's,

00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:06.844
you use criticism as a
kick not down, but forward.

00:02:06.844 --> 00:02:10.737
Use a no as motivation to
want to go and do better

00:02:10.737 --> 00:02:14.732
and eventually get the
yes, as opposed to, I suck.

00:02:14.732 --> 00:02:16.224
I'm never going to get this.

00:02:16.224 --> 00:02:17.623
This is never going to work out.

00:02:17.623 --> 00:02:19.982
I think it's really important
to be hard on yourself,

00:02:19.982 --> 00:02:23.912
but most people kick
themselves down instead of

00:02:23.912 --> 00:02:26.015
kicking themselves forward.

00:02:26.015 --> 00:02:27.932
So, if you can learn to use your no's

00:02:27.932 --> 00:02:30.192
as motivation to do better, then you're

00:02:30.192 --> 00:02:32.948
on the fast path to success.

00:02:32.948 --> 00:02:34.339
So, the question of the day today is,

00:02:34.339 --> 00:02:37.756
I'm curious, what was the most painful no

00:02:38.663 --> 00:02:41.110
that you've gotten in your life?

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:42.915
I want to know what it
is, so go in deep today.

00:02:42.915 --> 00:02:44.797
Leave it down in the comments below

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.668
and I want you to, as you write it out,

00:02:47.668 --> 00:02:50.786
release it so it doesn't
hold you back anymore.

00:02:50.786 --> 00:02:52.624
I'm curious, put it down in the comments.

00:02:52.624 --> 00:02:53.963
I also want to give a quick shoutout

00:02:53.963 --> 00:02:56.806
to Ed Reyes from Creative New Media, Inc.

00:02:56.806 --> 00:02:58.050
Ed, and thank you so much for picking up

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.215
a copy of my book, Your One Word.

00:03:00.215 --> 00:03:01.859
It really, really,
really means a lot to me

00:03:01.859 --> 00:03:04.465
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:03:04.465 --> 00:03:05.731
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:03:05.731 --> 00:03:06.886
I believe in you.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:09.000
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.112
and whatever your one word is.

00:03:11.112 --> 00:03:12.918
Much love and I'll see you again

00:03:12.918 --> 00:03:16.618
tomorrow morning for
another shot of Entspresso.

00:03:16.618 --> 00:03:19.201
(upbeat music)

00:03:24.952 --> 00:03:26.910
- Don't take no for an answer.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.548
If it's not working well that night

00:03:29.548 --> 00:03:31.668
it's not you, it's the audience.

00:03:31.668 --> 00:03:33.338
Blame it on the audience.

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:34.907
Because if it worked on Friday night,

00:03:34.907 --> 00:03:36.722
it ought to work on Saturday night

00:03:36.722 --> 00:03:38.735
and if it doesn't it's their fault.

00:03:38.735 --> 00:03:40.601
It's over simple but, you know what?

00:03:40.601 --> 00:03:42.213
It works as a formula.

00:03:42.213 --> 00:03:43.832
Just go on and do the Sunday night show

00:03:43.832 --> 00:03:45.908
and forget Saturday night.

00:03:45.908 --> 00:03:47.795
Keep kicking them in the nuts.

00:03:47.795 --> 00:03:49.078
- There's something about an entrepreneur

00:03:49.078 --> 00:03:52.745
that is somewhat sort
of anti-establishment,

00:03:53.915 --> 00:03:57.995
somewhat disrespectful of
the previous generation.

00:03:57.995 --> 00:04:00.931
And, although that can grate people

00:04:00.931 --> 00:04:02.545
the wrong way a little bit, that's a

00:04:02.545 --> 00:04:05.148
really important element actually.

00:04:05.148 --> 00:04:06.244
You need to be passionate about

00:04:06.244 --> 00:04:09.781
what you want to work on, and don't

00:04:09.781 --> 00:04:13.162
take no for an answer when people

00:04:13.162 --> 00:04:14.294
out there are giving you advice,

00:04:14.294 --> 00:04:15.293
and saying, no this'll never work.

00:04:15.293 --> 00:04:16.949
I mean, you look at eBay.

00:04:16.949 --> 00:04:20.032
The idea that in 1995, that you could

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:23.375
create something over the internet,

00:04:23.375 --> 00:04:24.955
which is this brand new thing that nobody

00:04:24.955 --> 00:04:27.108
was really using at the time, except for

00:04:27.108 --> 00:04:30.838
scientists and academics, and it would be

00:04:30.838 --> 00:04:32.817
a place where strangers could actually

00:04:32.817 --> 00:04:34.324
do business with on
another, could actually

00:04:34.324 --> 00:04:38.491
exchange merchandise for
money without ever meeting.

00:04:40.149 --> 00:04:41.144
And, the notion that people could

00:04:41.144 --> 00:04:44.041
actually buy and sell
cars over the internet.

00:04:44.041 --> 00:04:45.059
I mean, that's crazy, right.

00:04:45.059 --> 00:04:46.039
It's totally crazy.

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:47.507
So, in fact lots and lots of people

00:04:47.507 --> 00:04:48.947
told me that it was crazy.

00:04:48.947 --> 00:04:51.255
And, I didn't, I have to say I didn't

00:04:51.255 --> 00:04:52.720
have this vision, oh, it's going to

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:53.572
take over the world.

00:04:53.572 --> 00:04:55.971
It's going to be this
fantastic thing overnight.

00:04:55.971 --> 00:04:58.635
I didn't have that kind
of delusion, either.

00:04:58.635 --> 00:05:00.378
But, I did say, you know what?

00:05:00.378 --> 00:05:02.466
There's something worth trying here.

00:05:02.466 --> 00:05:03.716
It's not crazy.

